<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagerō Nikki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 974</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as The Gossamer Years, The Mayfly Diary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagerō Nikki, known in English as The Gossamer Years, is a work of classical Japanese diary literature written around 974 by a Heian noblewoman known only as the Mother of Michitsuna. Mixing waka poems and prose, it records her marriage to the courtier Fujiwara no Kaneie, her anguish as his visits to her dwindled, her pilgrimages, and her devotion to her son and adopted daughter. She probably began it around 971, during one of her marriage crises. Its frank first-person exploration of feeling expanded the possibilities of kana prose and influenced later women's writing, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagero-nikki.jpg" alt="Kagerō Nikki" width="330" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa, Kunisada, 1786-1864, artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Udaisho_michitsuna_no_haha_LCCN2008660907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Prominent Heian families often gathered their poems into a family collection, a kashū, and Fujiwara no Kaneie probably asked his wife to make one. By adding her own experiences to the poems she and Kaneie had exchanged, she produced instead a work of nikki bungaku, diary literature. The author, born in 935 as the daughter of the provincial governor Fujiwara no Tomoyasu, married Kaneie in 954 at nineteen. Kaneie rose to Minister of the Right and Regent, but as a secondary wife with one child she remained insecure, and the marriage ended after sixteen years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The diary turns on her marriage and on her son, the boy, and later her adopted daughter, a child of Kaneie's by another woman. She considers becoming a nun but holds back for her son's future, and at last accepts the separation. The first half reads more as memoir, the second as day-to-day entries, sometimes weeks or months apart, and she labels people sarcastically, as with that splendid personage of Machi Alley, Kaneie's lover. The work ends at the festival of the souls' return in the new year, with a late-night knock at her door by a visitor never named. Its title may mean a mayfly, a heat haze or the thin film of cobweb that Arthur Waley proposed.</p><p>The diary is the first work of literature to set out Heian social customs clearly. Husbands and wives often lived apart, the wife in her parents' house, and a marriage ended when the visits stopped; it also records Tōnori's proposal to marry the author's adopted daughter, his own niece.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Edward Seidensticker, whose first English translation gave it the name The Gossamer Years, called it a strikingly frank personal confession, and Donald Keene a self-portrait devastating in its honesty. In her translation of 1997 Sonja Arntzen read it as the record of a marriage's death and praised its realism and awareness of its own fictiveness. Some of the author's poems entered the Shūi Wakashū and Fujiwara no Teika's Ogura Hyakunin Isshu, and the work is often called the first autobiography written by a woman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kagerō Nikki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wrote instead in the new kana script; the result was much of the vernacular literature of the age: the <b>Kagerō Nikki</b> by “the mother of Michitsuna”, the Pillow Book of Sei Shōnagon, an attendant of the empress, and the Tale of Genji by Murasaki Shikibu, herself a Fujiwara.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
